<main class="wrap">

  <p class="kicker">Part IV · Can it run without you? · Chapter 20</p>
  <h1>Managing People</h1>
  <p class="lede">Three months in, you know. Not that the person cannot do the work — they can — but
  that it is not going to work, and you have started arranging your week so that you do not have to
  say so. The judgment was the easy part. What this chapter is about is the gap between the judgment
  and the conversation, which is where the money goes.</p>

  <div class="decision">
    <span class="tag">The decision</span>
    Where the date goes — the fixed point at which you must say <em>yes, permanently</em> or
    <em>no, and here is how we part</em> — and whether you set it when you hire, while you still
    can, or leave it to be set by the week you finally cannot stand it, when it will be later, costlier, and on Dutch ground a decision about a conversation the law has
    already shaped.
  </div>

  <section class="move">
    <h2><span class="num">Move 1</span> The situation</h2>

    <p>The first employee from <a href="19-the-first-hire.html">chapter nineteen</a> has been in the
    business a while now, or the second one has, and something is off. The work gets done. The
    person is capable — you checked that before you offered the job, and you would say so in a
    reference. But the day is heavier with them in it than it should be. Things you explained are
    explained again. The customers who used to ask for you still ask for you. The rest of the team,
    if there is one, has quietly routed around them. You have noticed all of this, and what you have
    done about it is give yourself another month.</p>

    <p>The month is not a plan. It is what an owner does when the decision has been made and the
    conversation has not, and it feels like fairness — <em>they deserve more time</em> — while
    actually being the opposite. The person is not being told what you already know. You are paying
    for work that is not what you need. And the part of your attention that should be on customers
    is on managing your own discomfort about a sentence you have not said.</p>

    <p>So the question is not <em>is this working</em>. You know. It is <strong>whether you have a
    date by which you are required to act on what you know</strong> — one that was set before the
    doubt arrived, so that it is not you who decides that this is the week, but the calendar.
    That is a different thing to build than a management style.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="move">
    <h2><span class="num">Move 2</span> What actually happened</h2>

    <p>In January 2015, three years into Groove, a small helpdesk-software company that published
    its revenue as it went, Alex Turnbull wrote a post about the part of the job he says he has
    always had trouble with. The figure he starts with is his own: <em>"But still, on a small team,
    a few employees is a lot. We've turned over nearly 40% of the team we launched with."</em> By
    his own account, that is the cost side of three years of hiring, and the post is about the part
    of it he calls his own.</p>

    <p>What he does not say is that they were bad hires. The opposite: <em>"I can confidently say
    that every single person I've hired at Groove has been more than qualified to do the job they
    were hired for."</em> The failure he owns is not one of skill. <em>"My own hesitance to spot poor fits and wait too long"</em> had cost, in his list:
    <em>"Our team taking longer to hit milestones that we should have because we weren't
    collaborating as well as we could have. Our growth slowing because unhappy employees weren't
    working at optimal productivity. My own productivity dropping because I was preoccupied with
    trying to force a fit where there was none."</em> Three costs, and the third is the one from Move
    1: the owner's attention, spent on a decision already made.</p>

    <p>What he built in response is a calendar, not a temperament. <em>"We always — at every level —
    start with a 2-4 week paid trial run where the prospective employee joins the team on a
    temporary (and sometimes part-time, depending on their current work situation) basis."</em> And: <em>"a trial is not a guaranteed job, but a paid
    consulting gig to see if there's interest from both of us in moving forward."</em> Which is to
    say, in chapter nineteen's terms, he buys a task before he offers a job — and he concedes the
    price of insisting on it: <em>"This might lock us out of some very talented people who might be
    excellent fits for Groove. But it's a non-negotiable for us."</em></p>

    <p>The trial's value is not the trial. It is that it ends on a date, and on that date something
    has to happen: <em>"That's why we do the trial period: the end of the trial — where we have to
    either sign a permanent contract or part ways — acts as a forcing function that makes the
    difficult conversation inescapable."</em> He does not claim the conversation got easier with
    practice. <em>"‘The conversation’ doesn't get any easier. Not the third time, the fifth time or
    the tenth time."</em> What changed was that it could no longer be avoided.</p>

    <p>Inside the trial, he describes what he does now, starting with the thing he had got wrong. On
    feedback: <em>"Early on, I used to wait until the trial period was over to give feedback. This
    helped nobody."</em> Now he gives <em>"direct and honest feedback often, even as often as daily
    during the trial period"</em>, and writes it down — <em>"After two weeks, you won't remember
    every small thing you said to someone. But if you took notes, you'll be able to easily spot the
    trends in your feedback, and separate the true strengths and weaknesses from the anomalies."</em>
    On whose judgment counts: <em>"I trust my team's (who works much more closely with the new
    employee) judgment even more than my own."</em> And on asking the person: <em>"I've had several
    people, when I asked them how they felt about their trial, tell me that they didn't think they
    were as productive at Groove as they could be elsewhere, or that they simply didn't think there
    was a fit."</em></p>

    <p>What transfers is the shape — a date set at the start, feedback during, other people's
    judgment before the decision — and the shape is what the rest of this chapter takes. What the
    trial ends is, in his words, <em>"a paid consulting gig"</em>; what ending an employment takes
    is a different question, and on Dutch ground it is the box's.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="move">
    <h2><span class="num">Move 3</span> The reasoning</h2>

    <h3>The date goes in before the doubt</h3>

    <p><strong>The expensive mistake in managing one or two people is not the wrong hire. It is the
    right judgment acted on late — and the remedy is a fixed date, agreed when the person starts, at
    which a decision has to be said out loud.</strong> Turnbull's costs are all costs of delay, and
    delay is what an owner produces when the only thing forcing the conversation is their own
    resolve. A date set in advance moves the decision off your willpower and onto the calendar, and
    it does something for the other person too: they know when they will be told, and that they will
    be.</p>

    <p>The boundary matters as much as the rule. The date is a forcing function for a decision about
    fit; it is not a licence to end things quickly in general, and it is not a way to avoid
    <a href="18-writing-it-down.html">chapter eighteen</a>'s finding — that a competent person will
    be slower than you for a while and that this gap is the transfer happening, not failing. A trial
    of two to four weeks judges whether the person and the work suit each other. It cannot judge
    whether they will be fast, and a business that ends trials for slowness will end all of them.
    And on Dutch ground the date is only partly yours: what a trial period can be, and what ending an
    employment after one takes, are in the box below.</p>

    <h3>Feedback during, in writing, from more than one person</h3>

    <p>Waiting for the end of the trial to say what you think <em>"helped nobody"</em>, and the
    reason is structural: a person who hears nothing for four weeks and then hears <em>no</em> has
    been denied the one thing the trial existed to give them, a chance to change. Say it as it
    happens. Then write down what you said — not for them, for you, because after two weeks you will
    remember the last thing that went wrong and not the pattern, and Turnbull's distinction between
    a trend and an anomaly is the whole difference between a fair decision and a mood. Chapter
    eighteen already gave you the instrument: the handoff test, with its two numbers, is feedback in
    this sense, and the person's corrections written into the document are theirs.</p>

    <p>The judgment should not be only yours. The person who works next to them sees more of them
    than you do, and Turnbull's <em>"even more than my own"</em> is not modesty; the owner sees the
    new hire least. And ask the person. Several of his told him it was not working before he did,
    which turned an ending into an agreement. That will not always happen, and the responsibility
    stays with you. But a decision the person has been asked about is a different event from one
    announced to them.</p>

    <p><em>Fit</em> — cultural, chemistry, Turnbull's own word for it — is a real thing and a dangerous
    one, because it is the word under which an owner's discomfort with someone
    unlike them gets recorded as a finding. The defence is the one this book gives every judgment: <strong>if you cannot write down what is not working in terms of the work — what was
    asked, what was done, what it cost — you do not yet have a fit problem. You have a feeling</strong>,
    and a feeling is not a finding in your notes, and it is not the ground the box quotes.</p>

    <div class="ground">
      <h3>On Dutch ground · ending an employment</h3>

      <p>If you are not employing in the Netherlands, skip to Monday. If you are, read this before the conversation, not after it, because the date this chapter tells
      you to set is a date for a decision, not for a dismissal, and here the two are different
      acts.</p>

      <p><strong>There is no dismissal at will.</strong> Article 7:671 of the Civil Code: <em
      lang="nl">"De werkgever kan de arbeidsovereenkomst niet rechtsgeldig opzeggen zonder
      schriftelijke instemming van de werknemer, tenzij"</em> — and the exceptions that follow include termination during the trial period and permission
      <em lang="nl">"als bedoeld in artikel 671a"</em>, which that article confines to grounds a and
      b of article 669: <em lang="nl">"op grond van artikel 669, lid 3, onderdeel a of b, verzoekt
      hiervoor schriftelijk toestemming aan het Uitvoeringsinstituut werknemersverzekeringen"</em>.
      For the ground this chapter is about, the door is the court, article 7:671b: <em
      lang="nl">"De kantonrechter kan op verzoek van de werkgever de arbeidsovereenkomst ontbinden:
      a. op grond van artikel 669, lid 3, onderdelen c tot en met i"</em>. Outside those, the
      employee's written agreement is what article 671 names. Article 7:669 sets
      what the employer must have first: <em lang="nl">"De werkgever kan de arbeidsovereenkomst
      opzeggen indien daar een redelijke grond voor is en herplaatsing van de werknemer binnen een
      redelijke termijn, al dan niet met behulp van scholing, in een andere passende functie niet
      mogelijk is of niet in de rede ligt."</em> A reasonable ground, <em>and</em> no reasonable
      redeployment.</p>

      <p><strong>Unsuitability is a ground with conditions.</strong> Among the grounds, lid 3
      onderdeel d covers the case in this chapter — unsuitability for the agreed work, other than
      through illness — <em lang="nl">"mits de werkgever de werknemer hiervan tijdig in kennis heeft gesteld en hem in
      voldoende mate in de gelegenheid heeft gesteld zijn functioneren te verbeteren en de
      ongeschiktheid niet het gevolg is van onvoldoende zorg van de werkgever voor scholing van de
      werknemer of voor de arbeidsomstandigheden van de werknemer"</em>. Told in good time, given a
      real chance to improve, and not left untrained. Feedback given as it arises, and a record that
      it was given, are here close to a precondition of the ground existing; notes kept only for
      yourself are not notice to the person.</p>

      <p><strong>The trial period is statutory and short.</strong> Article 7:652: <em
      lang="nl">"De proeftijd wordt schriftelijk overeengekomen"</em>; on an indefinite contract
      <em lang="nl">"een proeftijd worden overeengekomen van ten hoogste twee maanden"</em>; and
      none at all where the contract <em lang="nl">"is aangegaan voor ten hoogste zes maanden"</em>.
      Groove's two-to-four-week paid trial is not this — it is a contract for a task before any job
      exists, and chapter nineteen's box applies to it.</p>

      <p><strong>Notice and severance.</strong> Article 7:672 lid 2: <em lang="nl">"De door de
      werkgever in acht te nemen termijn van opzegging bedraagt bij een arbeidsovereenkomst die op
      de dag van opzegging: a. korter dan vijf jaar heeft geduurd: één maand"</em>, rising with
      service. Article 7:673 lid 2: <em lang="nl">"De transitievergoeding is voor elk jaar dat de
      arbeidsovereenkomst heeft geduurd gelijk aan een derde van het loon per maand"</em>, pro rata
      for part-years, and <em lang="nl">"bedraagt maximaal € 102.000,00 of een bedrag gelijk aan ten
      hoogste het loon over twaalf maanden indien dat loon hoger is dan dat bedrag"</em>. The cap
      <em lang="nl">"wordt telkens met ingang van 1 januari"</em> adjusted, so in a later year the
      figure above is stale by construction. The transitievergoeding is owed on a dismissal the
      employer initiates on a valid ground; it is not a fine for doing it wrong, and paying it does
      not supply the ground.</p>

      <p>None of this is advice. The box covers an employer ending an indefinite contract; a
      fixed-term contract's expiry, a termination by agreement, illness, and every other ground in
      article 669 are outside it. Before the first conversation that could be heard as a dismissal,
      pay an <span lang="nl">arbeidsrechtadvocaat</span> for an hour with the contract and your
      notes in front of them.</p>

      <span class="checked">Checked 28 August 2026 against Burgerlijk Wetboek Boek 7 articles 652, 669, 671, 671a, 671b, 672
      and 673, consolidated text at 1 January 2026. Statute quoted, not paraphrased;
      how the UWV and the courts assess a ground in practice is not covered here.</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="monday">
    <h2>Monday</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Write the sentence you are avoiding, in terms of the work.</strong> What was asked, what was done, what it cost. If it will not go into those words, you have a feeling, not a finding — and the next four items wait until it does.</li>
      <li><strong>Put the date in.</strong> For anyone you hire from now on, the decision date goes in the offer, and the offer says what will be decided on it. For the person you already have, choose a date four weeks out, tell them today what will be decided then, and on Dutch ground call the lawyer before you say it.</li>
      <li><strong>Give one piece of direct feedback this week, the day it arises, and write one line to yourself each day you gave any.</strong> At the date, read the lines before you decide; act on the trend, not the last one.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask two people before the date: the one who works closest to them, and them.</strong> The question is the same for both — <em>how is it going, and what would you change?</em> — and the answers go in the notes beside yours.</li>
      <li><strong>On the date, say one of two sentences.</strong> <em>Yes, and here is what permanent looks like</em>, or <em>no, and here is how we part</em>. A third sentence — <em>let's give it another month</em> — is only allowed if you write down what will be different about the month, and set the next date before the conversation ends. On Dutch ground, the second sentence is the one you have already put in front of the lawyer, in the words they gave you.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <div class="owed">
    <p class="case-head"><span class="who">What would prove this chapter wrong</span></p>

    <p>What is owed is an owner who did this properly — set the date at the start, gave feedback
    as it arose, asked the team and the person, said <em>no</em> on the date — and was wrong: the
    person who would have come good in the fourth month, and did, somewhere else, at a business
    that had not built the forcing function. Turnbull's own account offers only the other side —
    people kept too long — and that is the natural shape of the evidence, since an owner who ended a
    trial on schedule and later saw the person flourish elsewhere is describing a judgment they got
    wrong, and does not write that up either. The asymmetry to hold in mind is the one his list
    makes: the cost of ending on the date is one good person lost, visible, and recoverable by
    hiring again; the cost of not having a date is three kinds of slow, invisible until you count
    them, and by his account paid for three years.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="reading">
    <h2>Reading</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><span class="src">Source ·</span> <a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20150316193110/https://www.groovehq.com/blog/how-to-fire-an-employee">Alex Turnbull, "Firing: How I Do the Hardest Thing as an Entrepreneur"</a>, Groove blog, 29 January 2015, Internet Archive capture of 16 March 2015 — the "nearly 40%" turnover figure, the three costs of waiting, the two-to-four-week paid trial and its framing as a consulting engagement, the forcing-function sentence, the feedback and note-keeping practice, and the team's and the candidate's part in the decision. The original blog is no longer served at groovehq.com; the archive is the only copy.</li>
      <li><span class="src">Source ·</span> <a href="https://wetten.overheid.nl/BWBR0005290/2026-01-01">Burgerlijk Wetboek Boek 7</a>, consolidated text at 1 January 2026, articles 652, 669, 671, 671a, 671b, 672 and 673.</li>
      <li><span class="src">Note ·</span> A founder's own contemporaneous account, three years in — the weaker evidence class this book uses for every founder post, attributed as "by his own account." The turnover figure is round and self-reported; "a handful" is the only count of dismissals given; the people let go appear only as he relays them — several who said the fit was not there, and some who told him later it had been for the best — and no figure shows the forcing function producing the faster milestones he says the delay had cost.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

