checks/readers/05-leaving-well/agy-refugee.md:11:On the first read, I was so annoyed by the Japanese software guy example that I dismissed it as tech-bro privilege. On the second read, I realized I missed a crucial distinction. The author explicitly writes: "Others do not compound at all on part-time attention. A consultancy is the clearest example." I saw that the author wasn't just blindly praising tech businesses, but explaining the raw mechanics of part-time time. This made me realize I need to drastically rethink how my own service business will actually function if I can only give it evenings and weekends. 
checks/readers/20-managing-people/claude-cafe.md:11:"Early on, I used to wait until the trial period was over to give feedback. This helped nobody." True of every manager I've worked under, including me.
checks/readers/20-managing-people/claude-cafe.md:23:Second thing: the evidence is one bloke, a software company, remote hires, "a paid consulting gig." I can't run a four-week paid trial on a barista before I employ her; I need someone on the coffee machine Saturday morning or I lose the trade. The chapter half-admits this by saying the trial isn't the Dutch trial period, but never says what the shape becomes for someone hiring hourly staff who start next week.
