checks/readers/20-managing-people/claude-lagos.md:4:The opening got me — "you have started arranging your week so that you do not have to say so" is exactly what I do when a girl I pay to package orders keeps mixing up sizes and I just start doing the packing myself at night. Then about a third of the way in it became a Dutch law textbook and I stopped being the reader. I have one part-time person, no contract, no lawyer, no calendar with dates in it, and by the end I felt like the book had walked out of my shop and into an office in Amsterdam.
checks/readers/20-managing-people/claude-lagos.md:20:Groove is a software company with a team and a helpdesk product, paying a stranger for a "2-4 week paid trial run." Paying someone for four weeks to find out if they fit is a rich man's tool. Here, the trial *is* the job, and if I pay for four weeks and say no, that money is gone and my stock did not move.
checks/craft/20-managing-people.md:88:671a and termination during the trial period. Outside those, the employee's written agreement or
checks/craft/20-managing-people.md:104:source): *"and the exceptions that follow include termination during the trial period and
checks/craft/20-managing-people.md:111:and "or a court", leaving *"the exceptions that follow include termination during the trial period;
checks/craft/20-managing-people.md:129:("Early on, I used to wait…"); team feedback and asking the employee are given as practice, not
checks/craft/20-managing-people.md:148:sentences the post keeps apart — "let a handful of employees [go]" and "turned over nearly 40%" —
checks/craft/20-managing-people.md:156:a lot. We've turned over nearly 40% of the team we launched with." By his own account, that is the
checks/craft/20-managing-people.md:236:the statutory trial period is short and has to be written into the contract, and after it the
checks/craft/20-managing-people.md:252:yours: what a trial period can be, and what ending an employment after one takes, are in the box
checks/craft/20-managing-people.md:300:"more than qualified", the three costs, the 2-4 week trial as "a paid consulting gig", the forcing
checks/readers/18-writing-it-down/agy-refugee.md:8:I felt completely excluded when the text shifted to the scale of the example: "growing Gymdesk from a one-person operation to a company with over 10 FTE." Ten full-time employees? I am sitting here in the Netherlands hoping I can eventually afford one part-time local student to help with Dutch customer service. The talk of "self-funded B2B SaaS" and a "development queue" feels alien when my risk tolerance is basically zero. It assumes a level of stability, capital, and market confidence that someone on a temporary protection visa simply does not have.
checks/readers/16-enough/agy-refugee.md:5:The line "if you do not name it the business will name it for you" is exactly what I fear; my Dutch language classes, family obligations, and local bureaucracy already consume me, and I cannot afford a business that eats whatever time is left. I also deeply appreciated the strict reliance on reality: "budgets are aspirations and statements are evidence." In my old accounting career in Kyiv, this was a given, but it is comforting to see it demanded here. Calculating the gap between what my household needs and what other income (like my partner's part-time job) provides is the exact survival math I need to measure my risk.
checks/readers/05-leaving-well/codex-dev.md:13:The distinction between businesses that compound on part-time work and those that sell the founder’s hours was useful. “What did this business earn last month from work I did more than sixty days ago?” is the best diagnostic in the chapter.
checks/readers/21-meetings-that-arent-theater/claude-cafe.md:21:The whole case study is nine people at screens in different countries. The chapter says so itself — "the post is silent on a team in one room" — which is honest, but honesty doesn't get me a case. I've got seven people, three of them part-time teenagers, arriving at different hours, one of whom will be elbow-deep in a bain-marie at ten past nine. "Same time every working day, at the start of it" — whose start? My chef's start is 7am, my front-of-house's is 11. That's not a small wrinkle, it's the whole problem, and it isn't mentioned once.
checks/readers/04-deciding-not-to/fold/critic-agy.md:5:The argument insists that pre-committing to a specific consequence is essential because the future founder is a "compromised judge," yet its own success story actively undermines this premise. Eran Galperin (Gymdesk) failed to write down a consequence, but "improvised under pressure" by taking part-time work, which ultimately saved his business. A strict reader who dutifully followed the book's template and pre-committed to a narrower consequence—like shutting down completely—might destroy a viable business, proving the future self with actual context can make better strategic pivots than the uninformed present self.

