--
188-
189-<p><a class="Tweetable" target="_blank" href="http://web.archive.org/web/20150316193110/http://twitter.com/home?status=%E2%80%9CA%20group%20of%20all-stars%20doesn%E2%80%99t%20necessarily%20mean%20a%20winning%20team.%20Chemistry%20matters%20more.%E2%80%9D%20%40alexmturnbull%20https%3A%2F%2Fwww.groovehq.com%2Fblog%2Fhow-to-fire-an-employee%20via%20%40Groove">A group of all-stars doesn’t necessarily mean a winning team. Chemistry matters more.</a></p>
190-
191-<h2>Four Steps to Evaluate Cultural Fit</h2>
192-
193-<p>We’ve made no secret that we don’t hire anyone to a permanent full-time contract right off the bat.</p>
194-
195-<div class="bb-note">
196:  <p>We <em>always</em> — at every level — start with a 2-4 week paid trial run where the prospective employee joins the team on a temporary (and sometimes part-time, depending on their current work situation) basis.</p>
197-</div>
198-
199-
200:<p>This might lock us out of some very talented people who might be excellent fits for Groove. But it’s a non-negotiable for us, something we’ve learned over three years of learning from our experiences to be essential. We’re very upfront about this with every new employee, and make it clear that a trial is not a guaranteed job, but a paid consulting gig to see if there’s interest from both of us in moving forward.</p>
201-
202-<p>Beyond that, there are four things that I’ve come to see as the best and most dependable indicators of cultural fit, and I’ve made these an important part of our hiring process today:</p>
203-
204-<h3>1) Know Upfront What the Person’s Goals Are.</h3>
205-
206-<p><a class="Tweetable" target="_blank" href="http://web.archive.org/web/20150316193110/http://twitter.com/home?status=%E2%80%9CNobody%E2%80%99s%20ultimate%20goal%20is%20to%20simply%20work%20in%20the%20job%20that%20they%E2%80%99re%20applying%20for.%E2%80%9D%20%40alexmturnbull%20https%3A%2F%2Fwww.groovehq.com%2Fblog%2Fhow-to-fire-an-employee%20via%20%40Groove">Nobody’s ultimate goal is to simply work in the job that they’re applying for.</a></p>
207-
208-<p>Every person will have their own bigger goals, whether they want to start a business of their own, become an executive in your company, have work/life balance so that they can focus their life on raising their family, work for a “dream” company someday, become an expert in a certain skill that they’ll be improving in their role, etc…</p>
--
214-<p>So during a trial period, I not only evaluate whether they’re a good fit for their role and the team, but whether I think their role at Groove can help them achieve their ultimate goals.</p>
215-
216-<p>It becomes pretty clear after a couple of weeks whether someone will be able to lead a team well, or be productive enough to clock out at 5PM every day to go back to their family, or get the chance to work on the specific projects they’ll want to work on at Groove.</p>
217-
218-<h3>2) Give Direct Feedback Often, and Keep Notes for Yourself.</h3>
219-
220-<p><img src="/web/20150316193110im_/https://www.groovehq.com/attachments/blog/how-to-fire-an-employee/notes.jpg" alt=""/></p>
221-
222:<p>Early on, I used to wait until the trial period was over to give feedback.</p>
223-
224-<p>This helped <em>nobody</em>.</p>
225-
226-<p>I’ve learned that giving direct and honest feedback often, even as often as <em>daily</em> during the trial period, can help you and the employee feel out how you work together and how the employee reacts to your notes.</p>
227-
228-<p>Another thing that helps here is recording the feedback for your own benefit. After two weeks, you won’t remember every small thing you said to someone. But if you took notes, you’ll be able to easily spot the trends in your feedback, and separate the true strengths and weaknesses from the anomalies.</p>
229-
230-<h3>3) Get Feedback From the Team.</h3>
--
252-<p>“The conversation” doesn’t get any easier.</p>
253-
254-<p><img src="/web/20150316193110im_/https://www.groovehq.com/attachments/blog/how-to-fire-an-employee/the-conversation.png" alt=""/></p>
255-
256-<p>Not the third time, the fifth time or the tenth time.</p>
257-
258-<p>But I’ve learned that  it’s so, SO important, if things aren’t going to work out, to fire quickly.</p>
259-
260:<p>That’s why we do the trial period: the end of the trial — where we have to either sign a permanent contract or part ways — acts as a forcing function that makes the difficult conversation inescapable.</p>
261-
262-<p>I’ve come to look at firing someone as fulfilling a responsibility to myself, our business and to the person I’m letting go, too.</p>
263-
264-<p>Nobody likes to get fired. But if a talented developer or designer can do better or be better elsewhere, then it’s in nobody’s best interest to keep them here.</p>
265-
266-<p>I’ve heard this feedback later on from employees I’ve let go; quitting is hard and uncomfortable. Getting fired forces you out the door and on to your next chapter. It really can be a good thing in disguise.</p>
267-
268-<p>Whether I’m saying this because I’m out to help people find new opportunities or because I need to justify something that takes a huge emotional toll on me, I’m not sure.</p>
